{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"effective-cleansable-gull","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Problem\nOn origin/main, a project-supplied CODE-REVIEW criterion's `applies_to` is **stored, never\nvalidated, and never read**. The criterion runs on every review regardless of what changed.\n\nDispatch path, verified end to end on origin/main @ 721cb7d6ac:\n1. `src/rebar/llm/workflow/gate_dispatch.py:427-447` — `_activated_code_review_project_criteria(repo_root)`\n   filters on exactly two predicates, `criterion_id.startswith(\"project.\")` and `exec != \"DET\"`.\n   It takes no changed-files argument and never reads `applies_to`.\n2. `gate_dispatch.py:499` — the result is passed as `project_criteria=` to `CodeReviewBatchRunner`.\n   No diff or file context reaches it.\n3. `src/rebar/llm/code_review/batch_runner.py:86-87` — appended to `active_criteria` when\n   `step_id == ROUND_A`. The only gate is WHICH ROUND, not WHAT CHANGED.\n4. The bypass shape: project criteria are appended AFTER `req.criteria` — the set the gate YAML's\n   `when:` expressions already resolved — so they structurally sidestep the workflow's membership\n   gating.\n\n**The silent-failure variant is the real harm.** `criteria/overlay.py:336` stores the overlay entry\nverbatim, so `applies_to` survives into `effective_routing()` and is retrievable. But\n`_validate_routing_entry` (overlay.py:190-291) never checks it — no shape check, no unknown-key\nrejection — and no code path reads it for a `project.` id. A project can write\n`\"applies_to\": [\"**/.github/workflows/**\"]`, get no error, see it in `effective_routing`, and have\nit do NOTHING. ADR 0074 makes this worse by documenting `applies_to` as a `criteria_routing.json`\nknob WITHOUT scoping it to built-in overlay ids, actively inviting the trap.\n\nConfirmed absent (adversarially checked, all negative): `glob_triggered_overlays`\n(`code_review/registry.py:256-265`) does glob-match `applies_to` but iterates the closed\n`OVERLAY_IDS` built-in enum, which a `project.` id can never join; `applies_to_globs`\n(`registry.py:236-240`) reads the PACKAGED `routing_index()`, so an overlay's glob field is invisible\nto it; `gates/code-review.yaml` `round_a` has 14 hardcoded overlay slots and no project slot, hence\nno `when:`; `overlay_union` computes only over `OVERLAY_IDS`; `prompts.select_reviewers` glob-matches\nthe reviewer catalog, not criteria routing, and is never called from the gate.\n\n**Asymmetry with plan-review corroborates it.** Plan-review DOES gate project criteria —\n`plan_review/orchestrator.py:302-306` calls `registry.project_trigger_fires(...)` and `continue`s on\nFalse, evaluator at `plan_review/det_gate_rules.py:165-208` (`text_all`, `file_impact_globs`,\n`file_impact_reason_re`), plus `applies_at` scrutiny at `plan_review/registry.py:450-460`. Both keys\nARE validated at load (`criteria/overlay.py:249-291`). This repo's own overlay exercises both.\nCode-review has no counterpart to either.\n\n## Scope — MINIMAL. Deliberately excludes the config-pointer layer.\nGate project code-review criteria on `applies_to` against the review's changed files:\n- `code_review/registry.py` — a helper returning a project criterion's globs from\n  `effective_routing(repo_root)` (NOT the packaged index), and a matcher returning True when the\n  glob list is EMPTY. Reuse the existing `_glob_match` -> `_engine_support.commit_impact.glob_match`\n  that the built-in path already uses.\n- `workflow/gate_dispatch.py` — `_activated_code_review_project_criteria` takes\n  `changed_files: Sequence[str] = ()` and drops a glob-gated criterion matching none; the call site\n  threads `prep.dc.changed_files`, the SAME list the `triggers` step glob-matches, so both trigger\n  paths agree.\n- `criteria/overlay.py` — add the MISSING shape validation for `applies_to` itself (see AC4).\n\n**Explicitly OUT of scope**, and this is a deliberate reversal of the abandoned change 1919:\nno `applies_to_config_key` indirection, no new `[code_review] ci_config_globs` config key, no\n`_config_schema.py` / `_config_sections.py` / `config.py` / `config_surface.json` changes, and no new\nvalidator in the SHARED cross-gate criteria layer for a key only code-review reads. Independent\nreview found that layer to be roughly half of 1919's diff and to fix no part of the defect: the\nportability argument it claimed is already satisfied, because `applies_to` in\n`.rebar/criteria_routing.json` is declarative JSON owned and edited by the project itself. The\nconfig pointer was a second override layer over an already-project-owned field.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A project code-review criterion with a non-empty `applies_to` is DISPATCHED when a changed\n      file matches a glob, and NOT dispatched when none matches — proven by a test driving the real\n      dispatch path, RED before the fix.\n- [ ] A project code-review criterion with an EMPTY or absent `applies_to` remains ALWAYS-ON.\n      `project.review-phase-boundaries` — this repo's only activated project code-review criterion,\n      which carries `\"applies_to\": []` — must be provably unaffected.\n- [ ] Globs resolve through `effective_routing(repo_root)`, never the packaged `routing_index()`,\n      and match against the same changed-files list the `triggers` step uses.\n- [ ] `applies_to` gains SHAPE VALIDATION at overlay load, so a malformed value (a bare string, a\n      non-list, a non-string element) fails LOUDLY instead of narrowing to `[]` and silently\n      reverting to always-on. This is the ordering 1919 got backwards — it shape-checked its NEW\n      config key while leaving the pre-existing silently-ignored key unvalidated.\n- [ ] No new config key, and no change to `src/rebar/config.py`, `_config_schema.py`,\n      `_config_sections.py`, or `tests/golden/config_surface.json`.\n- [ ] ADR 0074 records two things: that `applies_to` is honoured for project code-review criteria,\n      and the SEMANTIC COLLISION on the key — for a BUILT-IN overlay an empty `applies_to` means\n      \"escalation-only, never glob-fires\" (`registry.py:262`), while for a PROJECT criterion it means\n      \"ungated, always runs\". The project meaning is forced (project ids are not in `OVERLAY_IDS`, so\n      the base reviewer can never escalate to them, and \"empty = never run\" would make them dead) —\n      but one key with opposite meanings must be documented, not left to be rediscovered.\n- [ ] `make lint` and `make typecheck` pass; complexity ratchet shows no new or increased entries.\n\n## Provenance\nSplit from `unaroused-undead-ibisbill` (CLOSED as rejected) and Gerrit change 1919 (ABANDONED) per\noperator decision 2026-08-19. The criterion that change was built to support is rejected for this\nproject; this defect stands entirely on its own merit and is filed independently. Conditionally\napproved by the operator subject to independent confirmation, which is recorded above and was\nperformed against origin/main rather than the abandoned change's worktree.\n","id":"d343-47c6-5987-43e4","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Project code-review criteria ignore applies_to: stored, unvalidated, never read"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787180446822008001,"uuid":"07d7b27b-3a61-4a99-b91b-ee13e68440d2"}